.TH GETC 3
.SH NAME
getc, getchar, fgetc \- get a character from a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <stdio.h>

int
getc(stream)
FILE *stream;

int
getchar()

int
fgetc(stream)
FILE *stream;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B getc
returns the next character from
.IR stream .
.B getchar()
is
.BR getc(stdin) .
.B fgetc
does what
.B getc
does, as a function instead of a macro: it is slower, but it may be
passed as a pointer to a function, and its argument is evaluated only
once.
.PP
The result must be kept in an int, not a char, so that EOF can be told
apart from the character 0377:
.PP
.nf
	int c;

	while ((c = getchar()) != EOF)
		putchar(c);
.fi
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
The character, as an int from 0 to 255, or EOF at the end of the file
or after an error;
.BR feof (3)
and
.BR ferror (3)
tell which.
.SH SEE ALSO
gets(3), fread(3), scanf(3), ungetc(3), ferror(3), putc(3), stdio(3)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B getc
function first appeared in Version 1 AT&T UNIX,
.B getchar
in Version 2, and
.B fgetc
in Version 7.
.SH BUGS
Since
.B getc
is a macro, it evaluates
.I stream
more than once: an argument with side effects, as in
.BR getc(*f++) ,
does not work.
